    title: "Location",
    content: "Embu and Mbeere, combined make up Embu County. The County lies approximately between latitudes 0°8’ and 0° 50’ south and longitudes 37°16’ and 37°56’ east. The County shares boundaries with Tharaka-Nithi County to the north and east, separated by river Thuci. To the west is river Ruvingaci which marks the boundary with Kirinyaga County. The Tana River marks the boundary with Machakos County to the south west and Kitui County to the south.\n\nEmbu County was created by the new Constitution of Kenya (2010), combining the former districts of Embu East, Embu West, Mbeere North and Mbeere South. The districts are now Constituencies, respectively named Manyatta and Runyenjes (hereafter referred to as Embu), Mbeere North and Mbeere South (hereafter referred to as Mbeere). Located on the slopes of Mt. Kenya in eastern Kenya, Embu County covers an area of 2,818 sq. km, of which the two Embu constituencies cover 718 sq. km, while Mbeere is much larger, covering 2,099 sq. km.\n\nThere are similarities as well as differences between Embu and Mbeere areas of Embu County. These are clearly visible in the topography, climate, natural resources, agriculture, touristic features and economic activities. Thus, it is best to unpack Embu and Mbeere respectively, so as to bring out the true flavour of the exciting sites and untapped potentials of the County.",
