    content: "The rainfall patterns in Embu County have two rainy seasons, the long rains falling between March and June, while the short rains are experienced from October to December. There is a cold spell between June and August. The higher altitudes are on the windward side of Mt. Kenya, hence Embu is relatively wet with average annual rainfall of 1,495 mm, but it varies greatly from 2,200-2,500 mm in the Mt. Kenya forest to less than 800 mm in the lower altitudes around Kagaari.\n\nMbeere, on the other hand, is relatively dry, as rainfall also varies with proximity to Mt. Kenya. Annual rainfall ranges from 1100 mm per year at higher altitudes in areas close to Embu and steadily declines to about 500 mm in lower altitude areas close to Kitui. Moreover, the rainfall patterns are erratic and Mbeere suffers regular droughts. For instance, severe droughts are on record for the years; 1930, 1943, 1964/65, 1974/75, 1983/84, 1991/92, 1995/96, 1999/2000, 2002, 2004, 2006, 2009 and 2010/2011.\n\nDue to effects of climate change, droughts have become more frequent, while dry spells persist longer. Flooding is not a frequent occurrence in both Embu and Mbeere, but when it occurs, it affects roads, bridges, homes and farmlands.",
