    content: "Embu County brings together two ethnic communities of Embu and Mbeere. The County has a population of 516,212 residing in 131,683 households. The mean population density is 183 persons per sq.km. There are 227,286 registered voters, while poverty prevalence is estimated at 41% (2012 data). The County has had a rapid population growth. Colonial era reports show that in 1933, the population of Embus was 37,516 and Mbeeres 23,335, adding up to 50,851 for the then Embu District. This is one-tenth of the population in 2012.\n\nThe major ethnic groups in Embu County are the Embu (Aembu) and Mbeere (Ambeere) people, who speak a similar language but with different dialects. Other ethnic groups include the Kikuyu, Kamba, Meru, Chuka, Tharaka as well as people from other parts of Kenya. The Aembu and Ambeere have been described as cheerful, intelligent, hard working, friendly, relatively straight forward and trusting. They can also be quite decisive, and this is sometimes confused with being stubborn. They are also welcoming and accommodative of visitors.",
