    title: "Origins of the Embu and Mbeere",
    content: "The Embu and Mbeere peoples are of Bantu extraction. Historians have indicated that the Embu and Mbeere, together with the Chuka, Kikuyu, Ndia and Meru people emigrated from Central Africa during the Bantu migration around the sixteenth century. It is believed that the Embu and Mbeere first settled in Meru, around Igembe and Tigania in the Nyambene Hills. The two groups began moving south into what is now Embu and Mbeere, around the seventeenth century. They first settled at a place called Igambango’mbe (meaning where cows moo) near Ishiara market, before they crossed Thuci river. Igambango’mbe is now within Tharaka-Nithi County.\n\nFrom Igambango’mbe, the Embu took a northerly course towards Mt. Kenya, while the Mbeere took a southerly one towards Kiambere hill. As a result, the two tribes share cultural bonds and traditionally, they jointly made sacrifices at Igambango’mbe especially on traditional “handover” ceremonies. The Mbeere even today remember Igambang’ombe as the place where sacrificial blessings should be “collected” since they were left there at the time of the crossing. Some Mbeere people remained at Igambango’mbe and still live there to date.",
