    content: "Oral history has an alternative explanation for the origins of the Embu people. One of the most widely accepted versions is the story of Mwene- Ndega, the first Embu ancestor, who lived with his wife Nthara in a grove at a place called Mwene-Ndega near the present day town of Runyenjes. The first two children of Mwene-ndega and Nthara were a boy named Kembu and a girl called Werimba, although there’s confusion about whether she was Kembu’s sister or daughter. Whatever, the two committed incest and were expelled from their forest home.\n\nKembu and Werimba moved away and settled near Karungu, where they lived as man and wife. Other daughters of Mwene-Ndega and Nthara, and those of Kembu and Werimba, later married and set up homes throughout Embu. Their descendants became known as the children of Kembu, or Embu for short. The importance of daughters and the naming of clans with female names suggests that the Embu people could have been originally matriarchal. It is also accepted that Ndega is the ancestral name of the Embu community, after the first Embu ancestor who was called Mwene- Ndega. The Embu people are sometimes referred to as “mbarῑ ya Karega”, in respect of this ancestral lineage.",
