    content: "An alternative explanation of the origins of the Mbeere claims that the earliest Mbeere people came from a place called “Marigūrῑ”, which means “Banana Grove”. This place was far away, beyond Igembe and was also known as “Rūkanga by the East”. From here, the Mbeere came together with the Embu to their right and the Kamba of Kitui to their left, crossing the river Thuci at Igambang’ombe. For this reason, the Kamba and Embu people are regarded by the Mbeere as their cousins.\n\nYet another plausible version has it that some, if not all, of Mbeere’s ancestors came from the south, from the direction of the coast. They came seeking a peaceful place after a battle with the Kamba of Kitui. As they moved northwards, the Mbeeres were ahead of the Kamba. They crossed the Tana river and climbed up the Kiambere hill, where they lit a fire and settled. Seeing the smoke column from a distance, the Kamba gave up the chase and said “ūsu mūmbele”, which in the Kamba language means “there! is the one in front”, hence the name Mūmbeere – Mbeere person. The word “Mbeere” or “Mbere” means ahead, first or in front. The River Tana to date marks the border between the Mbeere and Kamba people. The two tribes have several similarities in culture and language, and they used to trade. Generally, there is no claim of a single ancestor for the Mbeere people. It is therefore possible that the Mbeere came from both east and from the south or the coast.",
