    title: "Recent History of Embu-Mbeere Administrations",
    content: "The first European (comba, mūthūngū or mūcūngū), to set foot in Embu/ Mbeere was Johann Ludwig Krapf, the German missionary, who reached Mbeere when he crossed the Tana River in 1851. But real European influence arrived in 1906, after the British conquered the Embu at the battle of Ngoire. Thereafter, British colonial rule was set up in Embu in 1908 as a\n\nmilitary base, which was answerable to an administrative station at Fort Hall (now Murang’a).\n\nWhen Kenya became a colony in 1920, Embu was administered as part of the Kikuyu Province. At the time, Kikuyu Province consisted of five African Districts (Kiambu, Fort Hall, South Nyeri, Embu and Meru), and two Districts for European settlers (Nairobi and North Nyeri). In 1933, Kikuyu Province was merged with Ukamba (Machakos and Kitui) to form a larger Central Province. Thus the then Central Province consisted of territories occupied by the Embu, Mbeere, Meru, Kamba, Ndia, Gicugu and Gikuyu native peoples.\n\nEmbu District was at the time administered by a white District Commissioner (DC), with headquarters in Embu town (then called Kirimari or Embu Boma). The District had five divisions: Embu, Mbeere, Ndia, Mwea and Gichugu, each administered by a white District Officer (DO). At independence in 1963, Ndia, Mwea and Gichugu Divisions were hived off to create Kirinyaga District leaving Embu and Mbeere as the sole Divisions of Embu District. Embu also became part of Eastern Province, whose headquarters was at Embu town. Thus, for about 90 years (1906-1996), Embu and Mbeere were governed together as Embu District, by a common District Commissioner.\n\nThere were boundary realignments within Embu District in 1977, when Mbeere was subdivided creating Gachoka and Siakago Divisions, while Embu town became a municipality. Later, Embu Division was also sub- divided into Manyatta and Runyenjes, but all the Divisions remained aprt of Embu District.\n\nIn 1996, Embu District was subdivided into two, creating Embu and Mbeere Districts respectively. These were subsequently subdivided into four districts of Embu East, Embu West, Mbeere North and Mbeere South in 2009. The promulgation of the New Constitution of 2010 created Embu County, and the four districts became constituencies. These are Runyenjes, Manyatta, Mbeere North and Mbeere South respectively. The Embu County Government was institutionalized after the General Elections of 2013, in line with the new Constitution.",
