    content: "Coffee (kaūa or kaūga) is a major export crop in Embu, grown in the upper middle altitudes zones, about 1,400 metres above sea level, on the slopes of Mt. Kenya. Nearly all the coffee is grown by small holders in Embu, and there is no coffee in Mbeere.\n\nCoffee was introduced in Embu in 1933, but it was not until the 1960s that large numbers of farmers adopted the cash crop. The coffee boom of the 1970s saw much expansion of the land under coffee to the extent that there was little space left for other crops in upper Embu. The plummeting of coffee prices in the 1990s stemmed expansion of the crop, with farmers adopting integrated agricultural practices particularly growing of fruits and dairy farming. However, majority of the farmers in Embu still grow coffee.\n\nThere are several coffee factories in Embu, such as Ndunduri, Kapingazi, Kamurai and others. These factories do coffee pre-processing, such as pulping and drying. The superior Arabica coffee varieties are grown. The choice hand-picked coffee has the characteristic taste of Mt. Kenya coffees, giving it premium quality of international standards. Embu produced some of the finest coffees in the world.",
