    category: "food_and_livelihoods",
    title: "Millet and Sorghum",
    content: "Millet (mwere) and sorghum (mūvῑa) are dryland cereal crops grown mostly in the lower zones of Mbeere. Traditionally, both crops were the major food staples grown throughout both Embu and Mbeere. Millet and sorghum are used in preparing various foodstuffs such as porridge and ugali, as well as for local brewing. The millet crop is normally grown during the short rainy season (October-December). But traditional sorghum varieties take two rainy seasons to mature. Thus, sorghum is also planted during the short rains, but harvested in July-September. Modern sorghum varieties are short- maturing and are grown in one rainy season. Normally, millet and sorghum are grown together with legumes such as cow peas or pigeon peas. Finger millet (mūgῑmbῑ or mūkombi) is also grown in small quantities in some areas of Embu and Mbeere.\n\nIn recent years, the area under millet has been reducing due to changing dietary habits, as people switched to eating maize and other foods. Also, most farmers grow local millet varieties such as Kῑmbeere, Ciakaugi and Kῑraka which are low yielding. For sorghum, the yields average about 0.7 tons per hectare compared to 3 to 4 tons/hectare obtained from research stations.\n\nAnother deterrent to the growing of millet and sorghum is that both crops are attacked by grain eating birds which can cause considerable damage to the maturing crop. Thus bird-scaring is necessary during critical stages of crop growth and this requires labour and time input. Generally, farmers use traditional bird scaring techniques such as shouting, using noise making devices such as gourds, cans, drums, throwing stones with a sling or cracking whips (gῑkūtha). Traditionally, bird scaring was done by family members but with children going to school, family labour for the task is now unavailable.\n\nIn recent years, the Government has been promoting the growing of millets and sorghums in a project to revive “orphan crops”. However, the poor farm- gate prices fetched by these crops as well as the low yields discouraged adoption by farmers even in Mbeere. Opportunities such as growing sweet sorghum for industrial use should be explored.",
