    content: "Irish potatoes (waru) are not an indigenous crop and were introduced in Embu along with other agricultural reforms around 1962. The potatoes are grown in the higher rainfall areas of Embu in almost every farm. Although they have great economic potential, the production of Irish potatoes has been declining due to persistent pests and diseases, declining soil fertility and poor seed quality. As a result, Embu produces small quantities of Irish potatoes at subsistence scales.\n\nSweet potato (ngwacῑ) are grown for food in most parts of Embu, and the upper parts of Mbeere. Because they are difficult to store once harvested, sweet potatoes are grown in small quantities on small patches of land or on terrace banks. Although sweet potatoes can fetch good prices, they are not grown at commercial scales in Embu nor in Mbeere. Also, sweet potatoes have great potential as a strategic food security crop, since they do well on poor soils, but require adequate moisture.\n\nCassava (mwanga) is an important root crop which grows best with adequate rainfall, but can also survive dryland conditions. It is grown in both Embu and Mbeere as a famine insurance and strategic reserve food crop. Cassava is eaten raw, boiled, roasted or mixed with bananas and other foods in mashed dishes. Due to its association with famine relief, cassava is looked down upon as food for the poor, particularly in Embu. It is unthinkable to serve visitors with cassava in both Embu and Mbeere. Such negative perceptions are partly the reason cassava production has been declining. Cassava is one of the “orphan crops” promoted by the government to boost food security in the dry areas in the County.\n\nOther root crops grown particularly in Embu include arrow roots (ndūma) and yams (ikwa). Arrow roots have potential for commercialization as they are more expensive than all the other root crops grown in the County, they do well in Embu and mature quickly. However, this potential has remained largely untapped.",
